WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.400
You may have heard
the expression

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:04.240
“knowledge is power.”

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.040
Well, today we’re going
to give you more power

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.800
to control your
diet and lifestyle

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.960
by giving you the facts.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.760
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.240
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.160
Today we get the low-
down on a bacteria

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.760
most people have
never heard of.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:26.800
It’s called Helicobacter Pylori,
or H. pylori, for short.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.226
And it can cause big
problems in your stomach.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.560
For decades, Japan has had
the longest life expectancy

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.760
in the world, while spending
just a fraction on healthcare

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.000
compared to other
high-income countries.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.400
This longevity has been attributed
in part to Japanese dietary patterns,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.800
which are thought to have
contributed to their comparatively

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.960
low rates of coronary artery disease.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.560
Japan has historically
had amongst the lowest rates

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.080
of colon cancer, breast cancer,
ovarian cancer, prostate cancer,

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.000
bladder and blood cancers.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.280
Japan, however, has among the
highest rates of stomach cancer.

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:07.520
Yes, Japanese men may have had
seven times less prostate cancer

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.600
than Americans but got six
times more stomach cancer.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.040
Is there some Achilles' heel
in the Japanese diet?

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.080
One of the first theories,
proposed in the 1970s,

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.040
was that it was the talc
used to polish white rice

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.400
to give it a glossy sheen.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.320
That was the case with ovarian
cancer, which led to billions

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.360
in damages against Johnson
&amp; Johnson's baby powder,

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:32.480
but that did not appear to be
the case with stomach cancer.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:37.120
Is it just genetics? No.
Studies on Japanese migrants

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.200
show that as they and
their children Westernize

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:40.720
their diets and lifestyles,

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:42.960
their stomach cancer
rates drop accordingly.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:46.240
Well, the most well-established
risk factor for stomach cancer

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.760
is H. pylori, a bacteria that infects

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.440
the lining of the stomach and
causes the chronic inflammation

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:54.640
that can lead to cancer.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.440
H. pylori infection is considered
a Group 1 carcinogen, indicating

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.200
our highest level of certainty
that it indeed causes cancer.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.600
Korea and Japan have the
highest rates of stomach cancer

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.720
and among the highest
incidence of H. pylori infection.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.200
Case closed then, right?

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.760
The mystery seemed
to have been solved.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.680
But then came the African enigma.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.440
Countries such as Nigeria
had even more H. pylori,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.240
but only a fraction of Japan's
stomach cancer rates.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.600
Then came the Indian enigma.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.640
H. pylori is twice as prevalent in
India than Japan, yet Indians get

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:36.800
ten times less stomach cancer.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.040
Obviously, H. pylori alone
can't explain Japan's epidemic.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.520
Though most cases
of stomach cancer

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.680
are thought caused by H. pylori,

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.160
most people with
H. pylori don't get cancer.

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:53.200
H. pylori is one of the most
common human infections.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.040
It's been estimated that half
of the world's adult population

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.280
is infected with H. pylori, yet half
of us don't get stomach cancer.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.000
There must be some kind of co-factor
in countries like Korea and Japan

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.000
that explains their
elevated cancer rates.

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:12.480
The inflammation caused by
H. pylori may just set the stage

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.520
for cancer formation,
increasing the susceptibility

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.480
of the stomach lining
to dietary carcinogens.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.720
But what's so carcinogenic about
Korean and Japanese diets?

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.000
Studies that have compared
the dietary components

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.440
of different Asian populations
with similar H. pylori rates,

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.800
but dramatically different stomach
cancer rates, have suggested

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.080
preserved, salted foods—both fish
and vegetables—as the culprits.

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:41.120
Fresh vegetables and fruits, on
the other hand, were associated

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:44.480
with an 85% reduction in
stomach cancer odds,

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.440
whereas consumption of fresh
fish doesn't appear associated

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:48.800
with stomach cancer either way.

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:52.480
A review of 60 studies
found the consumption

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:55.840
of pickled foods was associated
with significantly higher rates

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:59.680
of stomach cancer, though
more so in Korea than Japan,

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:02.320
perhaps because per-capita
Korean consumption

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:04.960
of salt-fermented
vegetables like kimchi

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:06.560
is five to eight times greater.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:12.240
You can't know for sure though
until you...put it to the test.

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.920
Pickled vegetable extracts can cause
DNA damage in cells in a petri dish,

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.440
but what about in people?

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.440
Researchers in Vancouver fed
people 30 ounces of fukujinzuke —

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.680
assorted vegetables
pickled in soy sauce —

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:30.960
or pickled cucumbers
over a three-day period.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.880
Biopsies taken from their stomach
lining before the experiment started

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.480
were normal, as were
the biopsies taken after eating

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:40.240
fresh carrots or cucumbers.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:44.080
But after just a few days of
consuming pickled vegetables,

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:47.360
moderate to severe tissue
abnormalities were found,

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.080
suggestive of stomach irritation.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.600
The consumption of non-fermented
soy foods, such as tofu, edamame,

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.120
and soymilk is linked to a lower risk
of stomach cancer in Japan,

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.720
whereas no association was found
with fermented soy foods,

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:02.240
even highly salted miso.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.520
The protection afforded
by soy foods was attributed

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.000
to the anti-inflammatory
and anti-oxidant effects

00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:10.560
of the isoflavone
compounds in soybeans.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.680
Salt itself isn't considered
a direct carcinogen, but it may

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:19.520
damage the stomach lining,
thin the protective mucus layer,

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:23.600
enhance H. pylori colonization,
enhance the penetration

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.600
of carcinogens, and enhance
the formation of carcinogens.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.120
Even moderately high salt intake
is associated with significantly

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.400
increased risk of stomach cancer,
though in Japan this effect

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.120
may be limited to those
already suffering from

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:38.960
H. pylori-induced inflammation.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:44.880
H. pylori is typically treated with
a cocktail of multiple antibiotics.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:48.508
Is there any way to eradicate
it naturally through diet?

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.439
We'll find out next.

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:58.880
The salting and pickling of fish
produces a novel DNA mutating

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.840
chemical called CMBA, which is
formed from a reaction of the salt,

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.800
nitrite preservatives, and methionine,

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.920
an amino acid found concentrated
in animal proteins.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.440
The nitrites can also interact
with other protein components

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.960
to form N-nitroso compounds,
a powerful class of carcinogens

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.160
found in cigarette smoke.

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:22.160
This may explain why processed
meats, such as bacon, ham, hot dogs,

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:25.760
lunch meat, sausage, have been tied
to increased stomach cancer risk,

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:29.840
but this extends to fresh,
unprocessed, unsalted meat as well.

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:35.120
But wait. I thought most stomach
cancer was caused by an infection

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.600
with a stomach bacteria
called H. pylori.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:42.000
There's a synergistic interaction
between H. pylori-induced

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.120
inflammation gastritis and diet in
the formation of stomach cancer.

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:48.080
Check it out.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:51.520
Researchers in China discovered
that even genetically

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:54.960
vulnerable individuals
infected with a particularly

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.680
pathogenic strain of
H. pylori did not appear to be

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:05.440
at increased risk of stomach cancer
unless they ate about an ounce

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:07.040
or more of pork per day.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:09.520
An average pork chop
is like about six ounces.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:13.360
This is a striking example of how
our diet can sometimes trump

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:15.760
both our genes and
environmental influences

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:17.360
like cancer-causing infection.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.000
But is there a way to wipe out
the H. pylori in the first place?

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.400
Normally you'd use a triple
antibiotic cocktail of drugs

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.160
to kill off H. pylori, but patient
compliance is difficult to maintain

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.680
due to the quantity of drugs taken
and the adverse side effects.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:37.520
Anything we can eat
to wipe them out instead?

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:43.520
Decades before the detoxifying and
anti-cancer abilities were discovered,

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:48.080
sulforaphane, that remarkable
compound in cruciferous vegetables,

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.760
was originally described
for its antimicrobial activity.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:56.320
After hearing anecdotal reports of
individuals with H. pylori-induced

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:58.880
peptic ulcer disease
experiencing dramatic

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:02.720
and sometimes unexpected
relief after eating three-day-old

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:06.480
broccoli sprouts, researchers
at Johns Hopkins University

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:10.080
and elsewhere decided to put
broccoli sprouts to the test.

00:08:10.640 --> 00:08:14.800
Not only did broccoli sprout extracts
kill antibiotic-resistant strains

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.280
of H. pylori in a petri dish,

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:18.800
some patients who
were given as little as

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:21.600
a third of a cup of broccoli
sprouts a day for a week

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:24.560
were able to eradicate
their H. pylori infection.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:27.280
So how about a
randomized controlled trial:

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:30.080
broccoli sprouts vs. alfalfa sprouts?

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:36.000
And those given two to three servings
of broccoli a day worth of sprouts

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.560
were able to significantly cut down on
markers of both H. pylori colonization

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:42.480
and stomach lining inflammation.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:46.480
Though broccoli sprouts may
be able to eradicate H. pylori

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:48.400
in the majority of patients, 56%,

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:53.680
the standard triple drug antibiotic
therapy is much more effective,

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:56.320
about 90% eradication.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:00.000
Still, for those who don't meet
the criteria for drug treatment,

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.200
cruciferous vegetables may
present a safe, natural way

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:07.040
to combat H. pylori and the
development of stomach cancer.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:11.040
A compilation of 22 population
studies found that eating more

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:13.680
cruciferous vegetables
was associated with

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.800
a significantly lower
stomach cancer risk, but

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:22.480
broccoli has never been directly
put to the test, but garlic has.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:29.120
Observational studies dating back
to the 1960s on Japanese migrants

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:31.760
have suggested that allium
family vegetablesâ€”

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:34.720
garlic and onion family vegetablesâ€”

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:36.720
may be protective
against stomach cancer.

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:39.680
To date, there have been dozens
of such studies published,

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.560
and overall, eating lots of
allium vegetables was indeed

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:45.360
associated with significantly
lower stomach cancer risk.

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.080
There's evidence of publication bias
though, meaning there appears

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:53.520
to have been other studies that
maybe failed to show such an effect,

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:55.200
that were shelved
and never published.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:00.160
Even if this weren't the case,
observational studies never prove

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:01.120
cause and effect.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:04.960
Maybe low garlic and onion
consumption didn't contribute

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:06.480
to stomach cancer, for example,

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:11.600
but rather stomach cancer contributed
to low garlic and onion consumption.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:16.160
Decades of H. pylori stomach
inflammation leading up to the cancer

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:20.800
may have led to individuals choosing
bland diets to avoid discomfort.

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:26.080
You can't know if garlic really
helps until you put it to the test.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:30.800
Louis Pasteur was evidently
the first to describe

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:33.680
the antibacterial effect of
onion and garlic juices.

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:37.520
Petri dish studies have shown that
garlic is effective in suppressing

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:40.960
the growth of H. pylori at
concentrations achievable

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:43.200
in the stomach
with a single clove.

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:45.840
Even some antibiotic-resistant
strains are susceptible.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:50.640
But does this translate into
stopping the growth of cancer?

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:54.320
A randomized, double-blind,
controlled trial was launched

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:58.160
to find out. Thousands
of individuals at high risk

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:00.880
for stomach cancer
from 13 villages in China

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:03.280
were randomized
into various combinations

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:07.600
of antibiotics, garlic supplements,
and antioxidant supplements.

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:11.840
And just few weeks of antibiotics
led to a significant decrease

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:15.280
in subsequent precancerous
stomach growths 7 years later,

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:18.080
and a significant decrease in
subsequent stomach cancer

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:18.960
by 15 years.

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:23.760
What about the garlic?
No benefit by 7 years,

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:28.960
and only a non-statistically
significant reduction after 15 years.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:37.280
But in 2019 we got the 22-year update:
15 years after the study ended and

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:42.720
those who had taken the garlic did
indeed have a significantly lower risk

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:47.040
of subsequently dying from cancer,
though interestingly the protective

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:51.876
effect of garlic only seemed
to manifest among nondrinkers.

